{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VWW8D2JPhoaZzJ9JCkH3JU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe frame stagnation detection as window-level binary classification over agent trajectories.\n5\tSilver labels are generated via weak supervision from three labeling functions, then used to train and evaluate three detection approaches: structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n6\tFigure~\\ref{fig:pipeline} provides a system overview.\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_pipeline.pdf}\n11\t\\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}\n12\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n13\t\\end{figure*}\n14\t\n15\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n16\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n17\t\\label{sec:problem}\n18\t\n19\tGiven an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\n20\tThe task is to assign each window a binary label $y_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, where 0 denotes \\emph{stagnation} (the agent is stuck or unproductive) and 1 denotes \\emph{progress} (the agent is making meaningful headway toward the task goal).\n21\t\n22\tThe window formulation offers two advantages over alternatives.\n23\tCompared to step-level scoring, windows capture short-range temporal patterns---such as a sequence of repeated failed attempts---that individual actions cannot express.\n24\tCompared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\n25\t\n26\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}\n28\t\\label{sec:weak_supervision}\n29\t\n30\tNo gold-standard annotations exist for agent stagnation at the window level.\n31\tManual annotation is expensive: each window requires reviewing $k$ actions with full context (command text, outputs, file diffs), taking an estimated 2--3 minutes per window.\n32\tWe therefore adopt a data programming approach \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}: multiple labeling functions (LFs) provide noisy votes that are aggregated into silver labels.\n33\tEach LF $\\lambda_j : w_i \\to \\{0, 1, \\bot\\}$ examines a window and emits a vote: stagnation (0), progress (1), or abstain ($\\bot$).\n34\t\n35\t\\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}\n36\t\\label{sec:labeling_functions}\n37\t\n38\tWe design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_design}).\n39\t\n40\t\\paragraph{Command repetition ($\\lambda_1$).}\n41\tComputes the proportion of repeated commands within window $w_i$.\n42\tA high repetition ratio triggers a stagnation vote, capturing overt repetitive behavior such as re-running failing commands.\n43\tThis LF never abstains: it votes stagnation when the repetition ratio exceeds a threshold and progress otherwise.\n44\tIt votes stagnation on 65.5\\% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4\\% of OpenHands windows; the lower OH rate reflects OH's greater action diversity, which reduces command repetition.\n45\tOn OH, cmd\\_rep achieves 97.9\\% precision with 26.0\\% recall, and shifts from leading stagnation onset ($-1.87$ steps on SWE-Agent) to lagging ($+3.32$ steps), demonstrating agent-specific temporal dynamics (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{File diff delta ($\\lambda_2$).}\n48\tMeasures the fraction of steps within $w_i$ that produce code modifications (file creates, edits, or deletes).\n49\tA low modification frequency triggers a stagnation vote; windows with more than one edited file receive a progress vote; windows with exactly one or zero edits abstain ($\\bot$).\n50\tOn OpenHands, this LF achieves 87.5\\% recall on stagnation windows (precision 99.9\\%), making it the most reliable cross-agent signal.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$).}\n53\tUses the final task outcome (resolved/unresolved) as distant supervision.\n54\tFor unresolved tasks, the last two windows and any windows following a timeout or stuck signal receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes in the corresponding windows.\n55\tAll other windows abstain ($\\bot$).\n56\tUnlike $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, which operate on local window content, $\\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.\n57\tThis makes it a form of distant supervision: individually noisy, but complementary to the real-time signals when aggregated.\n58\t\n59\t\\begin{table}[t]\n60\t\\centering\n61\t\\small\n62\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n63\t\\toprule\n64\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Signal} & \\textbf{SWE Stag\\,\\%} & \\textbf{OH Stag\\,\\%} \\\\\n65\t\\midrule\n66\t$\\lambda_1$ (cmd\\_rep)    & Repetition & 65.5 & 5.4 \\\\\n67\t$\\lambda_2$ (file\\_diff)  & Output     & 57.3 & 17.8 \\\\\n68\t$\\lambda_3$ (outcome\\_bp) & Outcome    & 23.9 & 16.8 \\\\\n69\t\\bottomrule\n70\t\\end{tabular}\n71\t\\caption{Labeling function design. Stag\\,\\% = fire rate, i.e., fraction of windows on which the LF votes stagnation (remaining windows receive progress or abstain). SWE = SWE-Agent; OH = OpenHands. OH values reflect corrected action embeddings (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).}\n72\t\\label{tab:lf_design}\n73\t\\end{table}\n74\t\n75\t\\subsubsection{Label Aggregation}\n76\t\\label{sec:label_aggregation}\n77\t\n78\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:\n79\t\\begin{equation}\n80\ty_i = \\text{MV}\\bigl(\\{\\lambda_j(w_i) : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot\\}\\bigr)\n81\t\\label{eq:mv}\n82\t\\end{equation}\n83\tWe define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\\text{conf}_i = |\\{j : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot \\;\\text{and}\\; \\lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\\}| \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$.\n84\tConf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.\n85\tThe conf${\\geq}$2 subset, where at least two non-abstaining LFs agree on the winning label, serves as the primary evaluation set throughout this work.\n86\tThis stratification allows us to separately analyze model behavior on high-confidence vs.\\ ambiguous windows (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n87\t\n88\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n90\t\\label{sec:detection_methods}\n91\t\n92\t\\subsubsection{Structural Features}\n93\t\\label{sec:structural_features}\n94\t\n95\tWe extract a 30-dimensional feature vector from each window, requiring no pretrained model or GPU.\n96\tFeatures span four categories:\n97\t(1)~\\emph{repetition}: exact command repeat ratio, fuzzy match ratio (Levenshtein-based), and unique command ratio;\n98\t(2)~\\emph{file operations}: counts of file creates, modifications, and deletions within the window;\n99\t(3)~\\emph{edit patterns}: edit distances between consecutive actions, capturing how much the agent's behavior changes step-to-step;\n100\t(4)~\\emph{output statistics}: entropy of command outputs and action type distribution features.\n101\tThese features are fed to three classifiers: Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n102\tBecause several structural features overlap with the signals used by the labeling functions (e.g., repetition ratios relate to $\\lambda_1$), structural classifiers can partially reconstruct the LF voting process.\n103\tWe discuss this circularity and its implications in \\S\\ref{sec:main_results}.\n104\t\n105\t\\subsubsection{NL Embedding Probe (ProgressProbe)}\n106\t\\label{sec:progressprobe}\n107\t\n108\tEach action $a_t$ is encoded as a natural language description and embedded using Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} into a 1024-dimensional vector.\n109\tThe $k$ embeddings within a window form a sequence that is processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with hidden size 256 and dropout 0.1.\n110\tAn attention mechanism \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} computes a weighted combination of the GRU hidden states, yielding a single window-level representation.\n111\tThis representation is passed through a fully connected layer with sigmoid activation for binary classification.\n112\tTraining uses binary cross-entropy loss with early stopping (patience 5) on a validation set.\n113\t\n114\tThe GRU architecture is motivated by the sequential nature of actions within a window: the order in which commands are issued (e.g., repeated failures followed by a different approach) carries diagnostic information that bag-of-embeddings methods discard.\n115\tWe also evaluate two simpler embedding baselines to isolate the contribution of temporal modeling: (1)~mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression, and (2)~cosine similarity between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n116\t\n117\t\\subsubsection{LLM-as-Judge Baseline}\n118\t\\label{sec:llm_judge}\n119\t\n120\tWe prompt Claude Sonnet 4.6 in zero-shot mode to classify each window as stagnation or progress, given the textual descriptions of actions within the window.\n121\tThis baseline serves exclusively for evaluation---it is not used in the silver label pipeline and incurs \\$11.66 in API cost for the SWE-Agent test set alone, making it impractical for large-scale labeling.\n122\t\n123\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n124\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n125\t\\label{sec:temporal_models}\n126\t\n127\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}): $\\lambda_1$ leads by ${\\sim}2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ is near-concurrent, and $\\lambda_3$ lags by ${\\sim}7$ steps.\n128\tThis motivates exploring whether encoding temporal structure into label aggregation can improve over majority vote.\n129\t\n130\t\\subsubsection{Time-Varying Weights (TWV)}\n131\t\\label{sec:twv}\n132\t\n133\tTWV applies Gaussian kernel smoothing to each LF's activation sequence:\n134\t\\begin{equation}\n135\t\\tilde{\\lambda}_j(t) = \\sum_{t'} K_\\sigma(t - t') \\cdot \\lambda_j(t')\n136\t\\label{eq:twv}\n137\t\\end{equation}\n138\twhere $K_\\sigma$ is a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth $\\sigma$.\n139\tSmoothed activations replace raw votes for conf${=}1$ windows (where exactly one LF disagrees), potentially flipping labels in temporally ambiguous regions.\n140\tThe bandwidth $\\sigma$ controls the extent of temporal context: larger values incorporate more distant steps.\n141\t\n142\t\\subsubsection{Hidden Markov Models}\n143\t\\label{sec:hmm}\n144\t\n145\tWe model the label sequence as a Hidden Markov Model \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} with two hidden states (stagnation, progress) and observations defined by the joint activation pattern of the three LFs.\n146\tTransition and emission parameters are estimated via Expectation-Maximization (EM).\n147\tThe HMM captures temporal persistence---stagnation episodes tend to span multiple consecutive windows---which majority vote, operating independently per window, cannot model.\n148\t\n149\t\\subsubsection{Lag-Aware HMM}\n150\t\\label{sec:lag_hmm}\n151\t\n152\tThe standard HMM treats all LFs as synchronous.\n153\tLag-Aware HMM extends this by explicitly modeling each LF's temporal offset: $\\lambda_1$ is shifted by $-2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ by $-1$, and $\\lambda_3$ by $+7$, estimated from the temporal dynamics analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}).\n154\tTo prevent divergence, we apply two regularization strategies: (1)~\\emph{confidence anchoring}, which fixes labels for conf${\\geq}$2 windows and restricts EM updates to conf${=}1$ windows; and (2)~a prior strength of 5000 on transition parameters.\n155\tThese constraints keep the model close to the majority vote baseline while allowing it to resolve temporally ambiguous labels.\n156\tThe intuition is that aligning each LF's activation to its natural temporal role should produce cleaner emission estimates and, consequently, more accurate Viterbi decodings on ambiguous windows."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"b8ee6d6c-359f-4cfe-9791-e919cd5303cf","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:26:22.348Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017wR8ezhaDdHFZJJRjUHaAW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KDAEHyq45wpNto8ksEVadS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/experiments.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1651,"cache_read_input_tokens":16036,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"17f4e043-dd56-408a-940f-59059b3d9903"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KDAEHyq45wpNto8ksEVadS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\tWe evaluate stagnation detection across four axes: in-domain accuracy, cross-agent transfer, robustness to design choices, and temporal dynamics of labeling functions.\n5\tAll reported metrics are evaluated against silver labels derived from majority vote over three labeling functions (\\S\\ref{sec:method}).\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation caveat.}\n8\tStructural features (command repetition ratios, file operation counts, edit distances) are computed from the same signals that the labeling functions use to produce silver labels.\n9\tConsequently, the in-domain structural F1 of 0.995 reflects an upper bound on how well these features can \\emph{reverse-engineer} the labeling functions, not detection accuracy against true ground truth.\n10\tThe cross-agent evaluation on OpenHands (0.967 macro-F1) is non-circular: models trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions are tested on a different agent whose LF firing patterns differ substantially (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n11\tA human-annotated gold set provides the definitive non-circular evaluation (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n12\t\n13\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n14\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n15\t\\label{sec:setup}\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n18\tWe extract sliding windows of $k{=}5$ consecutive actions from agent trajectories.\n19\t\\textbf{SWE-Agent} trajectories from SWE-bench \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent} yield 54,809 windows with a stagnation ratio of 28.5\\%.\n20\t\\textbf{OpenHands} (OH) trajectories \\citep{wang2025openhands} yield 150,652 windows with a stagnation ratio of 21.4\\%.\n21\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote over three labeling functions; confidence equals the number of agreeing LFs (1--3).\n22\tUnless stated otherwise, evaluation uses the conf${\\geq}$2 subset: $N{=}1{,}824$ for SWE-Agent (485 stagnation, 1,339 progress) and $N{=}43{,}376$ for OH.\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{table}[t]\n25\t\\centering\n26\t\\small\n27\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrc}\n28\t\\toprule\n29\t\\textbf{Agent} & \\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{Windows} & \\textbf{Stag\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Conf${\\geq}$2} \\\\\n30\t\\midrule\n31\tSWE-Agent & SWE-bench & 54,809 & 28.5 & 15,401 \\\\\n32\tOpenHands & CodeAct   & 150,652 & 21.4 & 43,376 \\\\\n33\t\\bottomrule\n34\t\\end{tabular}\n35\t\\caption{Dataset statistics. Stagnation ratios differ substantially across agents, reflecting different action distributions.}\n36\t\\label{tab:dataset_stats}\n37\t\\end{table}\n38\t\n39\t\\paragraph{Detection methods.}\n40\t(1)~\\textbf{Structural features}: 30 hand-crafted features (repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances, output entropy) fed to Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n41\t(2)~\\textbf{ProgressProbe}: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} embeddings (1024-dim) processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with attention \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} (hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1), early-stopped at epoch~3 with patience~5.\n42\t(3)~\\textbf{Mean-pool + LR}: Mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression.\n43\t(4)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 4.6 zero-shot window-level classification (\\$11.66 API cost for the SWE test set).\n44\t(5)~\\textbf{Cosine similarity}: Cosine distance between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n47\tMacro-F1 is the primary metric. We also report per-class F1 for stagnation (F1\\textsubscript{stag}) and progress (F1\\textsubscript{prog}).\n48\t\n49\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n50\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n51\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n52\t\n53\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents in-domain results on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set and cross-agent transfer to OpenHands.\n54\t\n55\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n56\t\\centering\n57\t\\small\n58\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}\n59\t\\toprule\n60\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{SWE-Agent In-Domain} (conf${\\geq}$2)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{OpenHands Transfer}} \\\\\n61\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n62\t\\textbf{Method} & Macro-F1 & F1\\textsubscript{stag} & F1\\textsubscript{prog} & All & Conf${\\geq}$2 \\\\\n63\t\\midrule\n64\tStructural RF$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & 0.9123 & \\underline{0.9959} \\\\\n65\tStructural GB$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & \\textbf{0.9674} & \\textbf{0.9918} \\\\\n66\tStructural LR$^\\dagger$       & 0.9874 & 0.9815 & 0.9933 & 0.7801 & 0.9671 \\\\\n67\tProgressProbe (GRU)           & \\underline{0.9055} & 0.8593 & 0.9518 & 0.598$^a$ & 0.598$^a$ \\\\\n68\t\\quad + full OH fine-tune      & ---    & ---    & ---    & 0.976$^b$ & 0.976$^b$ \\\\\n69\tMean-pool + LR                & 0.8435 & 0.7620 & 0.9250 & ---    & --- \\\\\n70\tLLM-as-Judge                  & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & ---    & --- \\\\\n71\tCosine Similarity             & 0.5606 & ---    & ---    & ---    & --- \\\\\n72\t\\bottomrule\n73\t\\end{tabular}\n74\t\\caption{Stagnation detection results. In-domain evaluation on SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set ($N{=}1{,}824$) and zero-shot cross-agent transfer to OpenHands. $^\\dagger$Structural metrics are evaluated against LF-derived labels and reflect LF-reconstructibility rather than ground-truth accuracy (see circular evaluation caveat). $^a$Probe zero-shot macro-F1 is identical on all-windows and conf${\\geq}$2 subsets (0.598 to three decimal places). $^b$Probe fine-tuned macro-F1 confirmed on conf${\\geq}$2 via paired bootstrap with GB ($p{<}0.001$); all-windows value from the same model. Best in \\textbf{bold}, second-best \\underline{underlined}.}\n75\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n76\t\\end{table*}\n77\t\n78\tIn-domain, structural RF achieves 0.995 macro-F1, exceeding the GRU probe by 8.96\\,pp.\n79\tHowever, the structural--probe gap is inflated by circular evaluation: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels.\n80\tThe more informative comparison is among non-circular methods: the GRU probe outperforms mean-pool~+~LR by 6.2\\,pp, confirming that temporal modeling within windows adds value beyond static embedding aggregation.\n81\tThe zero-shot LLM judge (0.663) underperforms all learned methods despite substantially higher cost (\\$11.66 per test set).\n82\t\n83\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n84\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n85\t\\label{sec:cross_agent}\n86\t\n87\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} (right columns) shows models trained on SWE-Agent and evaluated on OpenHands without any OH training data.\n88\tStructural GB transfers best, achieving 0.967 macro-F1 on all OH windows.\n89\tRF drops to 0.912 on all-confidence OH data despite matching GB in-domain, suggesting GB's boosted ensemble is more robust to distributional shift.\n90\tBoth structural models recover to $>$0.99 on conf${\\geq}$2 OH windows, where label noise is lower.\n91\t\n92\tThe probe's zero-shot transfer (0.598) lags structural GB by 36.9\\,pp, but full OH fine-tuning closes this gap dramatically: the fine-tuned probe reaches 0.976 macro-F1 on all OH windows, surpassing structural GB (0.967) by 0.9\\,pp (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n93\tThe asymmetry between structural and learned methods is thus stage-dependent: in the zero-data regime, structural features transfer naturally while learned embeddings do not (+36.9\\,pp gap); with target-domain fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features (+0.9\\,pp on all windows).\n94\tThe practical implication is a deployment trade-off: use structural features when no labeled data is available; invest in probe fine-tuning when annotation budget permits.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}\n97\tStructural RF: $0.995{\\pm}0.000$ in-domain (3 seeds, zero variance); GB cross-agent: $0.968{\\pm}0.002$.\n98\tThe probe shows higher variance: $0.919{\\pm}0.011$, though all seeds exceed 0.90.\n99\t\n100\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n101\t\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\n102\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Encoder ablation.}\n105\tReplacing Qwen3-0.6B with BGE-M3 \\citep{chen2024bgem3} or E5-large-v2 \\citep{wang2024e5} yields probe macro-F1 of 0.941 and 0.936, respectively (vs.\\ 0.906 for Qwen3).\n106\tAll encoders exceed 0.90, indicating the probe's temporal architecture, not a specific encoder, drives performance. Full results appear in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:encoder_ablation}.\n107\t\n108\t\\paragraph{Window size.}\n109\t$k{=}5$ is optimal (0.931 macro-F1); $k{=}3$ drops 5.9\\,pp and $k{=}7$ drops 1.8\\,pp.\n110\tShorter windows lack sufficient context; longer windows dilute signal by spanning both progress and stagnation phases.\n111\tThe 0.931 figure differs from the main table's 0.906 because the window ablation uses a separate train/val/test split (seed~42, Qwen3-0.6B encoder) to enable controlled comparison across window sizes; cross-split variance accounts for the gap.\n112\tDetails in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:window_ablation}.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Data scaling.}\n115\tFigure~\\ref{fig:data_scaling} compares data efficiency across methods.\n116\tStructural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\\% training data, fully saturating by 50\\%.\n117\tThe probe scales monotonically from 0.805 (10\\%) to 0.906 (100\\%) but has not plateaued---a 10.1\\,pp drop that is roughly 20$\\times$ the structural degradation (${\\sim}$0.4--0.8\\,pp).\n118\tThis data efficiency further supports the structural signal thesis: the features capture patterns so regular that minimal training data suffices.\n119\t\n120\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n121\t\\centering\n122\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}\n123\t\\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers (RF, GB) saturate by 50\\% data; the probe scales monotonically but remains 9\\,pp below at 100\\%.}\n124\t\\label{fig:data_scaling}\n125\t\\end{figure}\n126\t\n127\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n128\t\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\n129\t\\label{sec:lf_temporal}\n130\t\n131\tWe analyze when each LF first fires relative to the onset of stagnation episodes (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_temporal}, Figure~\\ref{fig:lf_temporal}).\n132\t\n133\t\\begin{table}[t]\n134\t\\centering\n135\t\\small\n136\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n137\t\\toprule\n138\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Lag} & \\textbf{Lead\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Conc.\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Lag\\,\\%} \\\\\n139\t\\midrule\n140\tcmd\\_rep      & $-1.87$ & 36.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n141\tfile\\_diff    & $-1.20$ & 17.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n142\toutcome\\_bp   & $+6.70$ & ---  & --- & 48.4 \\\\\n143\t\\bottomrule\n144\t\\end{tabular}\n145\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions on SWE-Agent. Lag = mean steps relative to stagnation onset (negative = leading). Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\\times}10^{-60}$; all pairwise comparisons significant after Bonferroni correction.}\n146\t\\label{tab:lf_temporal}\n147\t\\end{table}\n148\t\n149\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches.\n150\tCommand repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps on average, making it an early warning signal.\n151\tFile diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$ steps).\n152\tOutcome backpropagation lags by 6.70 steps, acting as a retrospective confirmation.\n153\t\n154\tAll pairwise differences are significant (Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\\times}10^{-60}$; Bonferroni-corrected pairwise: cmd\\_rep vs.\\ file\\_diff $p{=}1.44{\\times}10^{-26}$, cmd\\_rep vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}6.67{\\times}10^{-39}$, file\\_diff vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}3.47{\\times}10^{-47}$).\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Cross-agent reversal.}\n157\tOn OpenHands, cmd\\_rep shifts from leading ($-1.87$) to lagging ($+3.32$ steps).\n158\tUnder corrected OH labels (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}), cmd\\_rep fires on only 5.4\\% of OH windows with 97.9\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall detector whose temporal role differs fundamentally from its SWE-Agent behavior.\n159\t\n160\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n161\t\\centering\n162\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_lf_temporal.pdf}\n163\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions relative to stagnation onset on SWE-Agent. cmd\\_rep leads ($-1.87$ steps), file\\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), outcome\\_bp lags ($+6.70$). Inset: OH reversal where cmd\\_rep becomes lagging.}\n164\t\\label{fig:lf_temporal}\n165\t\\end{figure}\n166\t\n167\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n168\t\\subsection{Label Quality and cmd\\_rep Coverage}\n169\t\\label{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}\n170\t\n171\tA label quality audit revealed that the OH embedding vectors used for silver labeling contained a version mismatch, causing systematic label errors.\n172\tAfter correction, the OH stagnation rate shifted from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%, with 21,898 window labels flipping.\n173\tThis section quantifies how label quality and cmd\\_rep coverage jointly affect probe transfer.\n174\t\n175\t\\paragraph{Label correction impact on probe transfer.}\n176\tWith corrected labels, the 3-LF probe improves from zero-shot 0.598 to fine-tuned 0.976 (previously 0.751 under corrupted labels).\n177\tThe improvement is most dramatic at low data fractions: 5\\% fine-tuning reaches 0.850, exceeding the old 50\\% result (0.69) by 16.0\\,pp.\n178\tThe probe converges rapidly: at 50\\% OH data it reaches 0.968, essentially matching structural GB (0.967) and demonstrating that the structural advantage is specific to zero-data deployment (full curve in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep}).\n179\tLabel quality, not model capacity, was the transfer bottleneck.\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{Prevalence-matched ablation.}\n182\tTo isolate cmd\\_rep's contribution, we compare 3-LF and 2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) models on the same test set with matched class prevalence.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n188\t\\toprule\n189\t\\textbf{LF Config} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\t3-LF (w/ cmd\\_rep) & \\textbf{0.9763} & 0.960 & 0.993 \\\\\n192\t2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) & 0.6822 & 0.481 & 0.884 \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\t$\\Delta$ & $-29.4$\\,pp & $-47.9$\\,pp & $-10.9$\\,pp \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\caption{Prevalence-matched ablation on the same OH test set ($N{=}6{,}122$, stag rate 19.3\\%). Removing cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$).}\n198\t\\label{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}\n199\t\\end{table}\n200\t\n201\tRemoving cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$), a statistically significant degradation driven primarily by stagnation recall ($-47.9$\\,pp F1\\textsubscript{stag}).\n202\t\n203\t\\paragraph{Coverage, not label quality, explains the gap.}\n204\tThe 29.4\\,pp drop is not caused by cmd\\_rep improving label accuracy.\n205\tAfter correction, cmd\\_rep fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows (down from the previously reported 92.2\\% under corrupted embeddings) with 97.91\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall signal.\n206\tOn the 4,070 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF labels are defined, the two configurations agree on 100\\% of labels: cmd\\_rep does not change any label where the other two LFs already vote.\n207\tIts contribution is purely \\emph{coverage}: including cmd\\_rep expands the training set from ${\\sim}$4K to ${\\sim}$43K windows (a ${\\sim}$10$\\times$ increase).\n208\tThe 2-LF model's low F1 reflects data volume collapse---too few training examples to learn the stagnation distribution---rather than label corruption.\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Implications for weak supervision.}\n211\tThese results reframe how labeling functions contribute to weak supervision pipelines.\n212\tA high-precision, low-recall LF like cmd\\_rep may appear redundant when its labels are a subset of the majority vote, yet removing it causes training data to collapse by an order of magnitude.\n213\tIn prevalence-matched evaluation, this data reduction manifests as a 29.4\\,pp F1 drop.\n214\tThe core contribution of such LFs is coverage provision, not label refinement: they extend the labelable portion of the input space rather than correcting other LFs' errors.\n215\t\n216\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n217\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n218\t\\label{sec:temporal_label_models}\n219\t\n220\tWe investigate whether incorporating LF temporal dynamics into label aggregation improves over majority vote (MV).\n221\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_models} compares three temporal label models \\citep{ratner2017snorkel,safranchik2020linkedhmm}.\n222\t\n223\t\\begin{table}[t]\n224\t\\centering\n225\t\\small\n226\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n227\t\\toprule\n228\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Conf${\\geq}$2 F1} & \\textbf{MV Agr.} & \\textbf{Probe F1$^\\ddagger$} \\\\\n229\t\\midrule\n230\tMV (baseline) & 0.8946$^\\ast$ & 1.000 & 0.8468 \\\\\n231\tTWV ($\\sigma{=}2$) & \\textbf{0.9003} & 0.885 & 0.8211 \\\\\n232\tLag-HMM & 0.8936 & 0.820 & --- \\\\\n233\tHMM & 0.7502 & 0.685 & 0.6564 \\\\\n234\t\\bottomrule\n235\t\\end{tabular}\n236\t\\caption{Temporal label model comparison. $^\\ast$MV conf${\\geq}$2 F1 is a self-evaluation baseline (MV evaluated against itself on high-confidence windows). $^\\ddagger$Downstream: probe trained on each method's labels, evaluated on the full SWE-Agent test set ($N{=}8{,}101$) against MV labels.}\n237\t\\label{tab:temporal_models}\n238\t\\end{table}\n239\t\n240\tTWV achieves the highest conf${\\geq}$2 F1 (0.900, +0.57\\,pp over MV), but this gain does not propagate downstream: a probe trained on TWV labels scores 0.821 vs.\\ 0.847 for MV-trained ($-2.57$\\,pp).\n241\tTWV flips 14.1\\% of conf$=$1 labels, introducing noise that hurts probe training.\n242\tHMM overpredicts stagnation (29,176 stagnation vs.\\ 25,633 progress windows), degrading downstream performance to 0.656.\n243\tLag-HMM closely tracks MV ($-0.1$\\,pp) despite explicitly modeling temporal offsets, suggesting that the temporal structure captured by individual LFs does not improve \\emph{aggregation}.\n244\t\n245\t\\paragraph{TWV sensitivity.}\n246\tThe Gaussian kernel bandwidth $\\sigma$ is critical: $\\sigma{=}2$ is optimal (0.900); $\\sigma{=}1$ drops to 0.811 and $\\sigma{=}5$ drops to 0.808.\n247\tThis narrow operating range, combined with the downstream degradation, makes TWV impractical as a drop-in MV replacement.\n248\t\n249\tWe additionally evaluate FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, a closed-form triplet-based label model.\n250\tOn the conf${\\geq}$2 subset, FlyingSquid agrees with MV on 100\\% of labels---unsurprising, since high-confidence windows leave no ambiguity to resolve.\n251\tOn the full dataset (including conf${=}$1), agreement drops to 82\\%; all divergent labels are FlyingSquid flipping progress$\\to$stagnation, introducing systematic stagnation bias.\n252\tThis mirrors the HMM's overprediction pattern and further confirms that the 3-LF low-conflict regime provides insufficient signal for any label model---temporal or otherwise---to improve over MV.\n253\t\n254\tThese results constitute a negative finding: despite clear temporal structure in LF firing patterns (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}), no label model---temporal (TWV, HMM, Lag-HMM) or closed-form (FlyingSquid)---improves over simple majority vote for downstream stagnation detection.\n255\t\n256\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n257\t\\subsection{Gold Set Validation}\n258\t\\label{sec:gold_set}\n259\t\\goldset{Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending. Results will be added upon completion (expected 2026-05-17). This provides the non-circular ground truth evaluation for all methods.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"5ad3ed61-f886-4efc-9326-3917efb6b066","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:26:22.821Z"}
