{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019kNYDv9uuGCxqHKLGZhgKm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Analysis}\n2\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Circular Evaluation and Its Implications}\n5\t\\label{sec:circular}\n6\t\n7\tThe structural RF's 0.995 macro-F1 (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}) is a predictable consequence of feature--label overlap, not a detection achievement.\n8\tThe 30 structural features include repetition ratios and file operation counts---the same signals $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ consume to produce silver labels.\n9\tThis score therefore represents an upper bound on LF reverse-engineering fidelity.\n10\t\n11\tTwo evaluation settings break this circularity.\n12\tFirst, cross-agent transfer: GB trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions achieves 0.967 macro-F1 on OpenHands, where LF firing patterns differ substantially (e.g., cmd\\_rep votes stagnation on 5.4\\% vs.\\ 65.5\\% of windows).\n13\tThe model cannot memorize LF outputs when tested on a different agent's distribution.\n14\tSecond, a human-annotated gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}) will provide definitive non-circular ground truth.\n15\tUntil then, all in-domain structural metrics should be interpreted as LF-agreement scores rather than detection accuracy.\n16\t\n17\t\\subsection{Why Temporal Label Models Fail}\n18\t\\label{sec:why_tlm_fail}\n19\t\n20\tDespite clear temporal structure in LF activations (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}), no temporal label model improves downstream detection over majority vote (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n21\tThe root cause is low label conflict in the 3-LF regime: conf${\\geq}$2 windows dominate the training set, leaving few ambiguous labels for temporal models to correct.\n22\t\n23\tTWV targets only conf${=}1$ windows, flipping 14.1\\% of their labels.\n24\tThese flips introduce noise rather than corrections---downstream probe F1 drops by 2.57\\,pp relative to MV-trained.\n25\tHMM overpredicts stagnation (29,176 vs.\\ 25,633 progress windows), producing a distorted label distribution that degrades the probe to 0.656.\n26\tLag-HMM, despite explicitly modeling per-LF temporal offsets, tracks MV within 0.1\\,pp---the offset information captured by individual LFs (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}) does not translate into better aggregation.\n27\t\n28\tFlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, a closed-form (non-temporal) label model, exhibits the same limitation: it matches MV exactly on conf${\\geq}$2 windows and introduces systematic stagnation bias on the remainder.\n29\t\n30\tThree binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns, yielding too little conflict for any label model---temporal or otherwise---to exploit.\n31\tA richer LF set ($>$10 functions with higher disagreement rates) would be needed to create the ambiguity where advanced aggregation could add value \\citep{ratner2017snorkel}.\n32\t\n33\t\\subsection{Error Analysis}\n34\t\\label{sec:error_analysis}\n35\t\n36\tWe analyze errors of the ProgressProbe (GRU) on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set under the stagnation-positive convention (positive = stagnation throughout).\n37\tThe model misclassifies 131 of 1,824 windows (7.2\\% error rate): 85 false negatives (predicted progress, actual stagnation; 64.9\\% of errors) and 46 false positives (predicted stagnation, actual progress; 35.1\\%).\n38\t\n39\tThe recall bottleneck is the dominant failure mode: $P_\\text{stag}{=}0.897$ vs.\\ $R_\\text{stag}{=}0.825$.\n40\tMissed stagnation windows (FN) are not borderline cases---the model assigns them a mean stagnation probability of only 0.078 (i.e., high progress confidence of 0.922), with 73\\% receiving $>$0.9 progress probability.\n41\tThese windows feature agents performing diverse edits and varied commands that mimic progress patterns while making no effective headway, constituting a systematic blind spot.\n42\tFalse positives arise when agents repeat specific command patterns during genuine progress.\n43\t\n44\tLF conflict is a strong error predictor: 39.7\\% of error windows have at least one LF disagreeing, compared to a 31\\% base rate.\n45\tThe cmd\\_rep LF contributes the highest per-LF error rate (12.75\\%).\n46\tErrors concentrate in trajectory midpoints (10.2\\% error rate vs.\\ 6.5\\% at endpoints), where the boundary between exploration and stagnation is most ambiguous.\n47\tError mass is also non-uniform across trajectories: the two highest-error trajectories account for 18.3\\% of all misclassifications.\n48\t\n49\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Error Patterns}\n50\t\\label{sec:cross_agent_errors}\n51\t\n52\tWe extend error analysis to cross-agent transfer, evaluating the SWE-trained probe and structural GB on the full OH conf${\\geq}$2 set (43,366 windows).\n53\tThe probe misclassifies 19.9\\% of windows, while structural GB errs on only 0.13\\%---a 150$\\times$ gap that quantifies the distributional shift penalty for learned embeddings.\n54\tOf the probe's errors, 80.5\\% are false positives (predicting stagnation on progress windows), indicating that the probe systematically misreads OH action patterns as stagnation signals learned from SWE-Agent.\n55\t\n56\tThe two models' errors are nearly disjoint: only 0.4\\% of probe errors overlap with structural errors (31 of 8,643 probe-error windows).\n57\tStructural features, being agent-agnostic by construction, are immune to the embedding distribution shift that drives probe failures.\n58\t\n59\tThe probe's cross-agent errors are not borderline: 62\\% occur at high confidence ($>$0.9 or $<$0.1), ruling out threshold tuning as a remedy.\n60\tThe embedding space encodes SWE-Agent--specific stagnation patterns that confidently misfire on OH distributions, explaining why even 5\\% target fine-tuning (reaching 0.850 macro-F1) dramatically outperforms zero-shot transfer.\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Outcome Backpropagation as Distant Supervision}\n63\t\\label{sec:ob_analysis}\n64\t\n65\tOutcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) is a privileged signal that uses task outcome, unavailable at inference time.\n66\tA 3-seed split-isolated holdout removing $\\lambda_3$ yields macro-F1 of $0.770{\\pm}0.022$, a 7.7\\,pp drop from the 3-LF baseline of 0.847.\n67\t$\\lambda_3$ contributes 33.5\\% of conf${\\geq}$2 labels (5,158 of 15,401 windows).\n68\t\n69\tHowever, $\\lambda_3$ functions as a coverage expander rather than an independent corrector: across 43,402 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF configurations produce labels, their agreement is 100\\%.\n70\t$\\lambda_3$ never flips a majority vote---it only promotes conf${=}1$ windows to conf${=}2$ by adding a third agreeing vote.\n71\tThis suggests that additional real-time LFs targeting the same coverage gaps could replace the privileged outcome signal without sacrificing label quality."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"c99ff900-1c47-4983-a323-5269a2a5d994","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:26:23.347Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016GjLCd5eSUnar8ckPXRYFY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RcYHU3mcFCzPZ4iw1zgbeS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15553,"cache_read_input_tokens":17687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15553,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"d042e49a-6e03-4f0c-b746-430a35a10c21"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RcYHU3mcFCzPZ4iw1zgbeS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe presented a systematic study of progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents, comparing structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge approaches.\n5\tThree findings emerge.\n6\t\n7\tFirst, structural features dominate zero-shot cross-agent transfer.\n8\tThirty lightweight features achieve 0.995 in-domain and 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1, outperforming neural probes by 36.9\\,pp and zero-shot LLM judges by 30.4\\,pp.\n9\tWith full fine-tuning the probe reaches within 1\\,pp of the structural baseline (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967), but structural features remain the default for zero-data deployment.\n10\tThese features are also data-efficient, maintaining F1 above 0.987 with only 10\\% of training data.\n11\t\n12\tSecond, labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal dynamics.\n13\tCommand repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps on SWE-Agent but lags by 3.32 steps on OpenHands.\n14\tA label quality audit reveals cmd\\_rep is a high-precision detector (97.91\\% precision) whose core value is coverage: prevalence-matched ablation shows removing it collapses training data 10$\\times$ (43K$\\to$4K) and costs $-$29.4\\,pp (p$\\approx$0).\n15\tLabel version bias---not LF noise---was the dominant cross-agent transfer bottleneck.\n16\t\n17\tThird, no label model improves over majority vote.\n18\tFour label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) and one closed-form (FlyingSquid)---all fail to surpass majority vote, constrained by insufficient inter-LF conflict among three binary labeling functions.\n19\t\n20\tAgent monitoring should therefore prioritize lightweight structural features ({<}5\\,ms per window, no GPU) over embedding or LLM approaches for zero-data deployment; when labeled target-agent data is available, neural probes become competitive.\n21\tThe circular evaluation caveat applies to in-domain results; cross-agent transfer provides stronger validation.\n22\t\n23\tFuture work includes extending evaluation to non-software-engineering agent domains, online integration with early termination or strategy switching, scaling to {>}10 labeling functions to test whether temporal aggregation becomes useful, and validation against the pending 650-window gold set."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFJd79LtNJdZgUTNBL4aEW","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"e735458b-45ca-4e7d-9e55-8a9e1b532d37","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:26:27.113Z"}
