{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMYi446wRb6YCSdwtE1qJD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Analysis}\n2\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Circular Evaluation and Its Implications}\n5\t\\label{sec:circular}\n6\t\n7\tThe structural RF's 0.995 macro-F1 (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}) is a predictable consequence of feature--label overlap, not a detection achievement.\n8\tThe 30 structural features include repetition ratios and file operation counts---the same signals $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ consume to produce silver labels.\n9\tThis score therefore represents an upper bound on LF reverse-engineering fidelity (a feature overlap ablation in \\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap} quantifies this decomposition).\n10\t\n11\tTwo evaluation settings break this circularity.\n12\tFirst, cross-agent transfer: GB trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions achieves 0.967 macro-F1 on OpenHands, where LF firing patterns differ substantially (e.g., cmd\\_rep votes stagnation on 5.4\\% vs.\\ 65.5\\% of windows).\n13\tThe model cannot memorize LF outputs when tested on a different agent's distribution.\n14\tSecond, a human-annotated gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}) will provide definitive non-circular ground truth.\n15\tUntil then, all in-domain structural metrics should be interpreted as LF-agreement scores rather than detection accuracy.\n16\t\n17\t\\subsection{Feature Overlap Ablation}\n18\t\\label{sec:feature_overlap}\n19\t\n20\tTo quantify how much of the structural classifier's performance derives from LF-overlapping features versus residual signal, we partition the 30 features into 11 that directly overlap with LF inputs (5 cmd\\_rep-derived, 5 file\\_diff-derived, 1 outcome\\_bp-derived) and 19 residual features (edit distances, token statistics, action diversity metrics).\n21\tTable~\\ref{tab:feature_overlap} reports GB performance under systematic feature removal.\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{table}[t]\n24\t\\centering\n25\t\\small\n26\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n27\t\\toprule\n28\t\\textbf{Feature Set} & \\textbf{\\#Feat} & \\textbf{In-Dom.} & \\textbf{Cross-Ag.} \\\\\n29\t\\midrule\n30\tFull (all 30) & 30 & 0.995 & 0.967 \\\\\n31\tNo cmd\\_rep feats & 25 & 0.968 & 0.562 \\\\\n32\tNo file\\_diff feats & 25 & 0.985 & 0.659 \\\\\n33\tNo outcome\\_bp feats & 29 & 0.995 & 0.966 \\\\\n34\t\\midrule\n35\tNo LF-overlap (residual) & 19 & 0.952 & 0.566 \\\\\n36\t\\bottomrule\n37\t\\end{tabular}\n38\t\\caption{Feature overlap ablation (GB). In-Dom.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands zero-shot. Removing all LF-overlapping features costs 4.4\\,pp in-domain but 40.2\\,pp cross-agent.}\n39\t\\label{tab:feature_overlap}\n40\t\\end{table}\n41\t\n42\tTwo findings emerge.\n43\tFirst, residual features retain substantial in-domain signal: 19 features still achieve 0.952 ($-$4.4\\,pp), confirming that stagnation manifests in patterns beyond what the three LFs capture---edit distance plateaus, token-level repetition, and action diversity collapse all carry independent diagnostic value.\n44\tThe in-domain score of 0.995 is thus not purely circular; roughly 96\\% of detection performance survives LF-overlap removal.\n45\t\n46\tSecond, cross-agent generalization depends almost entirely on LF-overlapping features.\n47\tRemoving cmd\\_rep features alone collapses cross-agent F1 from 0.967 to 0.562 ($-$40.6\\,pp); the full residual set (0.566) performs near the majority baseline (${\\sim}$0.55).\n48\tThe features that transfer across agents are precisely those encoding universal stagnation signals---command repetition patterns and file operation dynamics---rather than agent-specific idiosyncrasies captured by residual features.\n49\tOne might object that LF-overlapping features merely reconstruct the label-generation pipeline rather than detecting real stagnation.\n50\tTwo observations counter this.\n51\tFirst, the cross-agent evaluation is itself non-circular: the SWE-Agent LF firing distribution differs drastically from OpenHands (e.g., cmd\\_rep fires on 65.5\\% vs.\\ 5.4\\% of windows), so a model that merely memorizes LF outputs would fail on the target agent---yet structural GB achieves 0.967.\n52\tSecond, if cross-agent success were an artifact of label reconstruction, removing LF-overlapping features should \\emph{not} degrade cross-agent performance (the residual features would reconstruct equally well); instead it collapses to 0.566, confirming that the LF-overlapping features encode genuinely transferable stagnation signals rather than pipeline-specific artifacts.\n53\t\n54\t\\subsection{Why No Label Model Improves Over Majority Vote}\n55\t\\label{sec:why_tlm_fail}\n56\t\n57\tDespite clear temporal structure in LF activations (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}), no label model---temporal or generative---improves downstream detection over majority vote (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n58\tThe failure spans five models with distinct inductive biases: TWV and Lag-HMM model temporal dynamics, HMM captures latent state transitions, while FlyingSquid and Snorkel MeTaL assume conditional independence.\n59\tAll fail for the same root cause: three binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns, and conf${\\geq}$2 windows dominate the training set, leaving insufficient inter-LF conflict for any aggregation method to exploit.\n60\t\n61\tThe failure modes differ by model class.\n62\tTemporal models either introduce noise (TWV: $-$2.57\\,pp downstream despite $+$0.6\\,pp self-evaluated) or overpredict stagnation (HMM: $-$14.4\\,pp).\n63\tGenerative models fare worse: FlyingSquid ($-$11.3\\,pp) introduces systematic stagnation bias, and Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp, 42.3\\% MV agreement).\n64\tBoth assume conditional independence among LFs---violated by the shared behavioral signals underlying our three functions---and lack sequential structure to recover from this misspecification.\n65\t\n66\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication.\n67\tA richer LF set (${>}$10 functions with higher disagreement rates) would be needed to create the ambiguity where advanced aggregation could add value \\citep{ratner2017snorkel}.\n68\t\n69\t\\subsection{Error Analysis}\n70\t\\label{sec:error_analysis}\n71\t\n72\tWe analyze errors of the ProgressProbe (GRU) on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set under the stagnation-positive convention (positive = stagnation throughout).\n73\tThe model misclassifies 131 of 1,824 windows (7.2\\% error rate): 85 false negatives (predicted progress, actual stagnation; 64.9\\% of errors) and 46 false positives (predicted stagnation, actual progress; 35.1\\%).\n74\t\n75\tThe recall bottleneck is the dominant failure mode: $P_\\text{stag}{=}0.897$ vs.\\ $R_\\text{stag}{=}0.825$.\n76\tMissed stagnation windows (FN) are not borderline cases---the model assigns them a mean stagnation probability of only 0.078 (i.e., high progress confidence of 0.922), with 73\\% receiving $>$0.9 progress probability.\n77\tThese windows feature agents performing diverse edits and varied commands that mimic progress patterns while making no effective headway, constituting a systematic blind spot.\n78\tFalse positives arise when agents repeat specific command patterns during genuine progress.\n79\t\n80\tLF conflict is a strong error predictor: 39.7\\% of error windows have at least one LF disagreeing, compared to a 31\\% base rate.\n81\tThe cmd\\_rep LF contributes the highest per-LF error rate (12.75\\%).\n82\tErrors concentrate in trajectory midpoints (10.2\\% error rate vs.\\ 6.5\\% at endpoints), where the boundary between exploration and stagnation is most ambiguous.\n83\tError mass is also non-uniform across trajectories: the two highest-error trajectories account for 18.3\\% of all misclassifications.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Error Patterns}\n86\t\\label{sec:cross_agent_errors}\n87\t\n88\tWe extend error analysis to cross-agent transfer, evaluating the SWE-trained probe and structural GB on the full OH conf${\\geq}$2 set (43,366 windows).\n89\tThe probe misclassifies 19.9\\% of windows, while structural GB errs on only 0.13\\%---a 150$\\times$ gap that quantifies the distributional shift penalty for learned embeddings.\n90\tOf the probe's errors, 80.5\\% are false positives (predicting stagnation on progress windows), indicating that the probe systematically misreads OH action patterns as stagnation signals learned from SWE-Agent.\n91\t\n92\tThe two models' errors are nearly disjoint: only 0.4\\% of probe errors overlap with structural errors (31 of 8,643 probe-error windows).\n93\tStructural features, being agent-agnostic by construction, are immune to the embedding distribution shift that drives probe failures.\n94\t\n95\tThe probe's cross-agent errors are not borderline: 62\\% occur at high confidence ($>$0.9 or $<$0.1), ruling out threshold tuning as a remedy.\n96\tThe embedding space encodes SWE-Agent--specific stagnation patterns that confidently misfire on OH distributions, explaining why even 5\\% target fine-tuning (reaching 0.850 macro-F1) dramatically outperforms zero-shot transfer.\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Outcome Backpropagation as Distant Supervision}\n99\t\\label{sec:ob_analysis}\n100\t\n101\tOutcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) is a privileged signal that uses task outcome, unavailable at inference time.\n102\tA 3-seed split-isolated holdout removing $\\lambda_3$ yields macro-F1 of $0.770{\\pm}0.022$, a 7.7\\,pp drop from the 3-LF baseline of 0.847.\n103\t$\\lambda_3$ contributes 33.5\\% of conf${\\geq}$2 labels (5,158 of 15,401 windows).\n104\t\n105\tHowever, $\\lambda_3$ functions as a coverage expander rather than an independent corrector: across 43,402 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF configurations produce labels, their agreement is 100\\%.\n106\t$\\lambda_3$ never flips a majority vote---it only promotes conf${=}1$ windows to conf${=}2$ by adding a third agreeing vote.\n107\tThis suggests that additional real-time LFs targeting the same coverage gaps could replace the privileged outcome signal without sacrificing label quality."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Aeku9pEd4XWQ9xx6ssXZdR","session_id":"5b47ecc9-15a3-49db-b5e0-220a3eb8fe8c","uuid":"9efbfe05-a78d-46e5-8b2e-484b362c3023","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:30:32.445Z"}
